ry and damage their business rivals  yeah it's interesting you mentioned the  United States and we know that Neil Bush  is heavily involved in in the campaign  and and we and the West have always been  good Fred  with cue wait how have international  relations being damaged by this and and  how could they be damaged in the future  well it's very unfortunate you may know  that the Emir of Kuwait has been ill and  sadly I don't think he's really very  aware of what's been going on here and  but despite the efforts not just the  extraordinary is it's not just the  Americans it's not just even if you like  the British because my law firm only a  strategy a representing along with  British barristers it's also the the the  Russians too who are involved and it's  been raised by the Russian Foreign  Minister about the treatment of their  citizen and yet despite this there is  this blockage which lies firmly I think  with the Attorney General and the  prosecutors and sadly to some of the  judges in the case and that's why we  have fire proceedings in the United  Nations in Geneva the Human Rights  Commission complaining of her treatment  about complaining about her arbitrary  detention and also complaining to the  Special Rapporteur about the  Independence of the judges and he is  actually tweeted just recently his  concern about what has happened in this  case because if the rule of law is  manipulated in this way then actually no  one is is safe yeah how would you sum up  the human rights of curated America well  as I said I think at the moment foreign  workers in Kuwait are vulnerable because  it's not just Marcia while she was in  prison she came across a number of women  unjustly accused many of them were  migrant workers and only yesterday a  report came out again with the the UN  Rapporteur on human trafficking  highlighting what is essentially a slave  bazaar that is being carried on among  domestic workers in Kuwait using Apple  apps using Facebook and girls as young  as 16 being promoted for sale  essentially as as chattels that can be  used with advice saying you know don't  don't give them their passports you know  don't give them any holidays  you can imagine how vulnerable these  women are the domestic workers or far  away from home and where Marsha was in  prison she befriended a lot of them and  tried to help them with their case but  frankly within the legal system is  stacked against you even someone as  Marsha who's so well-connected now and  has so many people on her side no one  seems to be listening unfortunately the  international community is now listening  and are raising questions about what  exactly is going on in Kuwait good  excellent we're running short of time  but it won't have escaped your notice we  do have a general election on the way  and I'm just interested in getting your  thoughts about what it's like to be in  the spotlight when a campaign gets  underway  well I myself have been campaigning in  general elections since I was 16 which  sadly it's quite a long time ago now and  I elections or so that they're an  amazing thing because you could never  know what's going to happen and I think  this election particularly no one really  knows what's going to happen because it  all comes down to an individual and  their votes and I know lots of people  say oh you know what does it matter my  votes not going to count but actually  every vote does count I think  particularly now when everything is so  uncertain that people casting their  votes really can make a difference so I  don't envy those who are out canvassing  and traipsing the streets in December  it's not not the the best of fun i  remember the 97 election the sun always  seemed to be shining and there was a  sense of excitement and you know a good  change was coming I fear that that's not  going to be quite the feeling in the  dark days of December you've talked  about the campaigning work you do for  women and I'm interested in knowing what  the atmosphere is like now compared to  when you were in Downing Street because  it does seem to become very toxic  doesn't it it's become very poisonous  and a lot of it is directed at women and  women politicians I think this is it's  really shocking that so many good women  women MPs of all parties have helped the  pressure  on them and their families has led to  many of them standing down and maybe  it's it's a bit tied in with again the  internet and what is happening and how  that rather than bringing people  together seems to be polarizing people  so whether you have the internet being  used to sell domestic workers in Kuwait  or the inter internet being used to make  you know horrendous threats of violence  and and rape and just unpleasantness to  to women MPs it does appear as though  people think they can do and say things  on the internet with impunity and which  they would never dream of doing face to  face yeah it's a very different time  actually Blair it's been a pleasure  thank you very much indeed for coming in  speak to us thank you for having me you  see thank you
 

